**`ExtractPrecompiledCharsMap` throws on legitimate normalizer steps inside a `Sequence`**

The normalizer walk handles only `Precompiled` and `Sequence`; every other type hits the `else` branch and throws:

```csharp
else
{
    throw new NotSupportedException($"Normalizer type '{type}' is not supported. Only 'Precompiled' and 'Sequence' normalizers are supported.");
}
```

Because the `Sequence` branch calls `ExtractPrecompiledCharsMap` on each inner normalizer, any sibling step that is not `Precompiled` triggers the throw. Real Unigram tokenizers commonly nest the precompiled map alongside other steps, for example:

```json
"normalizer": {
  "type": "Sequence",
  "normalizers": [
    { "type": "Nmt" },
    { "type": "Precompiled", "precompiled_charsmap": "..." },
    { "type": "Replace", "pattern": { "Regex": " {2,}" }, "content": " " }
  ]
}
```

With this input the method throws on the `Nmt` (or `Replace`) entry and the whole load fails, even though the `precompiled_charsmap` it needs is present and extractable. This rejects many valid `tokenizer.json` files.

The correct behavior here is to not throw: when walking a `Sequence`, skip inner steps that are not `Precompiled` rather than aborting, and only extract the precompiled map.

Should we have a tracking issue to support the normalizers that we currently not supporting yet?

**`post_processor` (TemplateProcessing) is not consulted for BOS/EOS behavior**

`CreateFromTokenizerJson` decides BOS/EOS purely from the `addBeginningOfSentence`/`addEndOfSentence` parameters and locates the tokens by guessing the names `<s>`/`</s>`. The `tokenizer.json` already declares this authoritatively in `post_processor`:

```json
"post_processor": {
  "type": "TemplateProcessing",
  "single": [
    { "SpecialToken": { "id": "<s>",  "type_id": 0 } },
    { "Sequence":     { "id": "A",    "type_id": 0 } },
    { "SpecialToken": { "id": "</s>", "type_id": 0 } }
  ],
  "special_tokens": {
    "<s>":  { "id": "<s>",  "ids": [0], "tokens": ["<s>"] },
    "</s>": { "id": "</s>", "ids": [2], "tokens": ["</s>"] }
  }
}
```

This template is the source of truth for which special tokens wrap the input, in what order, and with which IDs. By ignoring it, the loader cannot tell whether a model wraps with BOS/EOS by default, and for models whose BOS/EOS are not literally `<s>`/`</s>` (or that use only one of them) the name guessing will pick the wrong token or none at all.

Suggested direction: when present, read `post_processor.special_tokens` (and the `single` template) to resolve the BOS/EOS token strings and IDs, using the `addBeginningOfSentence`/`addEndOfSentence` parameters as the on/off override rather than as the sole source.

@tarekgh So I did some investigation and post_processor is actually a bit more involved, but we need it in order to have a complete story here.  

Pulling 57 popular models from HuggingFace that use SentencePiece, 51 use post_processor.  37 of them are simple with just 1 or less prefix and suffix, but 14 have multiple tokens. 

I'm spiking out a proper `post_processor` implementation, but would need your review and guidance before doing major changes.  As such I'll move this back to draft.

[src/Microsoft.ML.TimeSeries/SrCnnBatchAnomalyDetection.cs] Correct. I commented the same thing above before I noticed your comment. 
If you need the number of rows in the data, you can compute it here in the ctor, by getting a row cursor with no active columns and counting how many times `MoveNext()` returns true. So you can do something like this:
```
var rowCount = input.GetRowCount();
if (rowCount!=null)
    _batchSize = rowCount;
else
{
    _batchSize = 0;
    using (var cursor = input.GetRowCursor())
    {
        while (cursor.MoveNext())
            _batchSize++;
    }
}
```

